          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The rose directly presents her love for the little prince as true. The surrounding statement that he did not know it because of her fault frames this as a disclosure of an existing emotional state.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows love but does not itself undertake a future action, obligation, or continuing relationship. The rose's neighboring farewell instructions and relinquishment of the globe are separate acts and should not be transferred onto this expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The expression is represented as a calm, intelligible disclosure rather than a reflex-like affective outburst. The scene contains strong emotion, but that does not make the declarative avowal an expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The core force of the focal expression is adequately captured as an avowal of a loving state; no distinct residual function is evident in the exact expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During a farewell as the little prince prepares to leave, the rose calmly discloses that she loves him and says that her own fault prevented him from knowing it.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No commitment is undertaken by the focal expression. The rose's surrounding directives and acceptance of his departure are separate utterances. The supplied scene shows no explicit acknowledgment or acceptance by the little prince of the love avowal; he instead raises concerns about wind and animals.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"Recognised as Chapter IX of Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supports the represented avowal and supplies behavioral evidence of affection, including the rose's concealed tears, but it does not establish broader relationship terms or explicit uptake of the avowal.\"}"
